{% from "_macros.html.j2" import nation_table with context %}
{% set h = q1.home %}
<p class="close-kicker">Question 1</p>
<h1 class="slide-q">Is the Czech pool thin?</h1>
<p class="slide-a">Not per head. Czechia ranks <strong>{{ ordinal(h.rank_nhl) }} of {{ q1.n_nations }}</strong> for NHL players at {{ f2(h.nhl_per_million) }} per million, {{ q1.nhl_vs_median_word }} the peer median of {{ f2(q1.peer_median.nhl_per_million) }}; {{ q1.top_nhl.name }} leads at {{ f2(q1.top_nhl.nhl_per_million) }}.</p>
<figure class="figure-bleed" data-chart="q1-bars">
  {{ nation_table(q1.table, 'nhl', 'nhl_per_million', 'NHL players') }}
  <figcaption>How to read it: one bar per nation, players with at least the games threshold in {{ q1.season }} divided by the population in millions; the dashed line is the median of the nine peers. Switch to "NHL + rung 2" to add the SHL, Liiga, National League and DEL: Czechia has {{ h.top5 }} players there, {{ f2(h.top5_per_million) }} per million, {{ ordinal(h.rank_top5) }} of {{ q1.n_nations }} (peer median {{ f2(q1.peer_median.top5_per_million) }}).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="figure-bleed" data-chart="q1-series">
  <table class="fig-table">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Season</th><th scope="col" class="num">Czech NHL players</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{% for r in q1.home_series %}<tr><td>{{ r.season }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.n if r.n is not none else 'lockout' }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody>
  </table>
  <figcaption>How to read it: each line is a nation's count per million, season by season; Czechia is the blue line, the two peers picked in the chips are ink and grey. The NHL series starts in {{ q1.first_season }} with {{ q1.first_n }} Czech players, rises to {{ q1.peak.n }} in {{ q1.peak.season }} and falls to {{ q1.peak.latest.n }} in {{ q1.peak.latest.season }}; 2004/05 was not played. The rung 1 + 2 series starts in {{ q1.top5_first_season }}; seasons before {{ q1.complete_first }} are shaded because the DEL did not publish nationality, so they are lower bounds.</figcaption>
</figure>
<details class="fold slide-method" open>
  <summary>How we know</summary>
  <p class="slide-did"><span class="slide-did-label">What we did</span> For each nation and season we counted the distinct players of that nationality who reached the games threshold in the NHL (or in any of the five leagues), then divided by the population. The threshold is {{ q1.threshold_def }}: 20 games in a full NHL season, fewer in the short 2012/13 and 2020/21 seasons and in shorter European schedules.</p>
  <p class="slide-did"><span class="slide-did-label">Sensitivity</span> Counting any game at all, Czechia has {{ q1.sens_any.nhl }} NHL players ({{ f2(q1.sens_any.nhl_per_million) }} per million, {{ ordinal(q1.sens_any.rank_nhl) }}) and {{ q1.sens_any.top5 }} in the five leagues ({{ ordinal(q1.sens_any.rank_top5) }}). With a flat 20 games everywhere, {{ q1.sens_fixed.nhl }} and {{ q1.sens_fixed.top5 }} ({{ ordinal(q1.sens_fixed.rank_top5) }}).</p>
  <p class="slide-how"><span class="slide-how-label">Coverage</span> In {{ q1.season }} every qualifying NHL, Liiga and DEL player has a nationality; the National League has {{ q1.coverage.NL.unidentified }} of {{ q1.coverage.NL.qualifying }} unidentified (foreign licences with no match), the SHL {{ q1.coverage.SHL.unidentified }} of {{ q1.coverage.SHL.qualifying }}. Only {{ q1.n_complete }} seasons of the rung 1 + 2 series ({{ q1.complete_first }}–{{ q1.complete_last }}) have every rung-2 league at least 90% identified.</p>
</details>
